Let R's thread check accept a new thread after fork.

A strict-barrier build turns on THREADCHECK: R_check_thread() records
the first thread to call into R and R_Suicide()s when any other thread
does.  The harnesses run R on a thread of their own (harnesses/common.h),
and AFL++'s forkserver forks from the main thread after
LLVMFuzzerInitialize, so every child starts R a new thread.  That
thread's pthread_t matches the recorded one only if the thread library
happens to put it where the old one was.  glibc 2.31 and 2.39 do, by
handing it the dead thread's cached stack.  glibc 2.43 and macOS leave
the dead thread's memory mapped, the new thread lands elsewhere, and the
child's first call into R dies with "Wrong thread calling 'Rf_protect'",
as the fork check in tests/eval.c does there.

A child handler registered with pthread_atfork() clears the record, so
the first thread to call into R in the child becomes R's thread.  A
child forked from R's own thread, as parallel::mcfork() does, records
that thread again, so the check keeps its meaning there.

Instrumentation, not a fix: R itself only forks from its own thread.
Not for upstream.

diff --git a/src/main/memory.c b/src/main/memory.c
--- a/src/main/memory.c
+++ b/src/main/memory.c
@@ -3206,11 +3206,26 @@
 #ifdef THREADCHECK
 # if !defined(Win32) && defined(HAVE_PTHREAD)
 #   include <pthread.h>
-attribute_hidden void R_check_thread(const char *s)
+static Rboolean main_thread_inited = FALSE;
+static pthread_t main_thread;
+
+/* Fuzzing instrumentation: the harnesses run R on a thread of their own
+   and AFL++'s forkserver forks from another, so a child starts R a new
+   thread, which macOS and glibc 2.43 give a different pthread_t.  The
+   child's first caller becomes R's thread instead. */
+static void forget_main_thread(void)
 {
-    static Rboolean main_thread_inited = FALSE;
-    static pthread_t main_thread;
+    main_thread_inited = FALSE;
+}
+
+attribute_hidden void R_check_thread(const char *s)
+{
     if (! main_thread_inited) {
+        static Rboolean atfork_registered = FALSE;
+        if (! atfork_registered) {
+            pthread_atfork(NULL, NULL, forget_main_thread);
+            atfork_registered = TRUE;
+        }
         main_thread = pthread_self();
         main_thread_inited = TRUE;
     }
